      "writtenOn": "2021-09-02T05:06:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why this change ? I don\u0027t think it\u0027s a problem, but generally using the most concrete type possible is more informative, as long as it doesn\u0027t put unnecessary constraints on a caller.\n\nE.g. : If you have a method that takes a map, and it doesn\u0027t care what kind of map it takes because it only uses methods from Map, then taking a Map argument is better than taking a HashMap because clients have choice in what kind of Map is better for them. But for a local var or a member, if you already know it\u0027s a HashMap anyway, it tends to be better to type it as HashMap.\n\nBy the way, in Java it doesn\u0027t change anything at all, but if you ever do C++ for example, using the supertype also may lose you some optimizations.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 511,
        "startChar": 22,
        "endLine": 511,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "afdcf8a43c157a258a30c69676f73c35f958c95f",
